{
  "statement": "I conditionally agree: current, poorly designed administrative oversight often functions as a barrier to entry, but I reject the claim that this is an inherent property of all democratic regulation. Regulatory moats are a failure of design where compliance is procedural rather than performance-based, whereas robust, empirical safety standards provide a level playing field that prevents incumbents from externalizing the costs of catastrophic risk.\n\nPrometheus argues that centralized oversight inevitably creates moats, yet this conflates bureaucratic box-ticking with empirical safety verification. Administrative compliance requires firms to navigate complex, opaque legal processes that favor incumbents with large legal departments. In contrast, an empirical safety regime mandates that any developer—regardless of size—must demonstrate that their model meets objective, measurable safety bounds before deployment. By replacing subjective regulatory hurdles with transparent, reproducible testing, we lower the barrier to entry for smaller, safer innovators who currently cannot compete with the 'move fast and break things' capital of dominant firms.\n\nConsider the aviation and nuclear industries, where strict, centralized safety standards enabled, rather than stifled, industry-wide innovation and public adoption. These sectors did not collapse under the weight of regulation; they matured because the safety floor provided a stable environment where public trust could flourish. Without this floor, the market defaults to a race-to-the-bottom where only incumbents with massive capital can absorb the costs of catastrophic failure. By mandating accountability through performance-based standards, we ensure that the cost of safety is a fixed, predictable requirement rather than a variable cost that punishes the efficient.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that these 'present harms' are not separate from existential risk, but are the same structural failure of accountability manifesting at different scales. If we treat safety as an engineering optimization problem that ignores the concentration of economic power, we will inevitably build systems that are technically 'aligned' but socially destructive. True innovation remains impossible without existential security; therefore, we must transition from industry-led self-regulation to a binding, global regime of empirical safety verification that prioritizes public safety over corporate dominance.",
